## 1. Introduction
As one of the most widely used antibiotics, tetracyclines are extensively used in various aspects, such as bacterial infection, and agriculture and livestock products. It is reported that tetracyclines are the second largest used in the world, accounting for almost one third of the antibiotics’ production and consumption [1]. The extensive use of tetracyclines inevitably causes their environmental releases at substantial levels. It was measured that 0.8–6.8 $\upmu\mathrm{g/L}$ and $0.1{-}158~\upmu\mathrm{g/Kg}$ tetracyclines from the water and sediment samples of Nanming River in China [2], the highest concentration of tetracycline in soil from vegetable farmlands of the Pearl River Delta, southern China, were reported $74.4~\upmu\mathrm{g/Kg}$ [3]. mean tetracycline levels were even measured in the range $5.4\mathrm{-}8.1~\mathrm{ng/L}$ in surface and ground water in the area of Tehran [4]. Besides, $30{-}497~\mathrm{ng/L}$ of tetracycline were detected in all the three rivers of Hong Kong [5]. The approximate concentration of tetracycline in the wastewater was reported $2.375\upmu\mathrm{g/L}$ [6].
The presence of tetracyclines in environments readily leads to the development of antibiotic-resistant pathogens, thereby causing great threats to human health and ecological balance [7-9]. With the superfluous accumulation of antibiotics in the human body, they can induce nephropathy, affect kidneys and other mutations, such as mutagenic and teratogenic effects [10]. The existence of tetracycline in water environments will affect the composition and activity of microorganisms, which thereby changes the ecological structure of the microbial flora. In the respect of biotoxicity effect, tetracyclines can not only produce direct toxicity to microbes but also acute and chronic toxicity to the atypical species of algae, algae and crustaceans. Under anaerobic conditions, tetracyclines at $8.5~\mathrm{mg/L}$ exhibited a terminal/lethal effect on the microbial community and inhibited biogas generation and substrate/COD utilization, leading to a total collapse of the reactor [11].To minimize the risks of tetracyclines to ecological environments, it is necessary to remove them from both natural water bodies and wastewaters. To date, substantial efforts have been carried out in this field, and several methods such as adsorption, biological degradation, ion exchange, membrane filtration, and photocatalytic degradation have been tested and proposed for tetracycline removal [10, 12]. Among them, photocatalytic degradation of tetracycline considered the most favorable methods, due to its low cost but high efficiency, mild reaction conditions and no secondary pollution [13, 14]. It is reported that the photocatalytic oxidation possessed excellent performance in tetracycline removal [15].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) , constituted by metal ions (or clusters) and organic ligands, have already become a rapidly expanding field because of their considerable applications in adsorption and photocatalysis [16]. These materials have specific three-dimensional structures and a series of characteristics like the unique textural, physical and chemical properties, including inherent large surface areas, tunable but uniform cavities, and functionalized easily using different ligands [17, 18]. Among the massive MOFs reported in recent years, the MIL (MIL represents Material of Institute Lavoisier) series composed of trivalent metal centers and 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (H2BDC) ligand or trimesic acid ( $\mathrm{\bf~\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BDC})$ mainly including Fe-based MILs, Al-based MILs, Ti-based MILs and Cr-based MILs, have especially become a hot field [19-23].
Fe-based MILs are extremely attractive since iron is an earth abundant element and its price is very low. Fe-based MILs can also play a role as Fenton-like catalysts and have highly chemical and water stabilities [24]. In addition, Fe-based MILs are visible-light responsive because of the existence of iron-oxo (Fe-O) clusters, making the (Fe-O) clusters excitated possibly [25]. In the past years, several Fe-based MILs were used in catalysis and photocatalysis. For chemical catalysis, Kholdeeva et al. [26] investigated the abilities of Fe-based metal-organic frameworks MIL-100 and MIL-101 over Hydrocarbon oxidation. The highly selective benzene hydroxylation to phenol can be achieved over two Fe-based metal-organic frameworks (MIL-100(Fe) and MIL-68(Fe)) under visible light irradiations using hydrogen peroxide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ as an oxidant [27].The photocatalytic $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ reduction via $\mathrm{g}{}{}{\bf-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}/\mathrm{MIL}{}{}-53(\mathrm{Fe})$ heterojunctions was also studied and the composite showed high photocatalytic efficiency [28]. In the aspect of pollutant degradation, Li et al. [29]compared the adsorption and degradation ability of acid orange 7 (AO7) over four kinds of Fe-based MILs. Fe-based metal–organic framework/graphene oxide composites were prepared and used to degrade methylene blue and RhB under exposure to natural sunlight [30]. Besides, Zhao et al. [31] prepared $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/M I L–101}$ nanocomposite and used as an efficient heterogeneous catalyst for degradation of Rhodamine B (RhB) pollutants in Fenton-like system. Based on these applications and physical and chemical properties of Fe-based MILs, it is assumed that Fe-based MILs might also be used to absorb and/or degrade tetracycline from wastewaters. To date, however, no information is available to the feasibility of Fe-based MILs removing tetracycline.
The aim of this work is therefore to assess whether and how Fe-based MILs remove tetracycline. First, three typical Fe-based MILs, i.e., Fe-MIL-101, Fe-MIL-100 and Fe-MIL-53, were synthesized through hydrothermal method, and their adsorption time was optimized. Then, the removal performance of tetracycline among the three MILs was compared. Since Fe-MIL-101 exhibited the best performance in tetracycline removal, the mechanisms of how Fe-MIL-101 removes more tetracycline than the other two MILs were also explored. To achieve more information for its practical application, the effects of some key parameters such as the dosage of Fe-MIL-101 and the concentration of tetracycline on tetracycline removal were finally investigated. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work assessing the feasibility of Fe-based MILs serving as adsorbents and or catalysts for tetracycline removal. The findings obtained expand the application fields of Fe-MILs and clarify the details of how Fe-based MILs achieve tetracycline removal, which may have important implications to the removal of tetracycline or other antibiotics in the future.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1 Sources of tetracycline containing medium and chemical reagents
All chemicals and solvents used in the experiments were of analytical grade and used without additional treatments. The tetracycline, iron chloride hexahydrate $\mathrm{(FeCl_{3}.6H_{2}O\ )}$ ), N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and ethanol, ferric nitrate nonahydrate $(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , trimesic acid $\mathrm{\bf{H}}_{3}\mathrm{\bf{B}\mathrm{TC)}}$ , benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid ( $\mathrm{\bf~H}_{2}\mathrm{\bfB}\mathrm{\bfD}{\bf C})$ were all purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd., China. De-ionized water ( $\mathrm{18.25\:M\Omega2cm})$ was prepared from laboratory and used in the whole experiment. The tetracycline solution was prepared by dissolving tetracycline powder in deionized water to prepare a stock solution of $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ concentration. All experimental tetracycline solutions were prepared from the stock solution by dilution.
## 2.2 Preparation and of Fe-MILs
Fe-MIL-101 was synthesized by a hydrothermal method according to a previously reported procedure with some modifications [32]. Typically, $0.675\mathrm{g}$ 1 $(2.45~\mathrm{\mmol}$ ) $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0.206\mathrm{g}$ 1 $\mathrm{1.24~mmol}$ ) H2BDC were dissolved in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. The DMF solution was sonicated for fifteen minutes to make the solid fully dissolved; before being transferred to a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and heated at $110~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an oven for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ . The dark orange solid products were separated by centrifugation and purified with DMF and hot ethanol two times ( $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , 3h). Afterwards, the purified products were dried at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Lastly, the materials were activated in an oven at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $8\mathrm{h}$ .
The synthesis of Fe-MIL-53, was performed according to the method documented in the literature [33]. Briefly, equal molar ratio of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ were first dissolved in DMF solution. After ultrasonication for about $15~\mathrm{min}$ , the mixture was placed in a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave, heated at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . After the autoclave was cooled to room temperature naturally, the products were centrifuged at $6000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The obtained powders were suspended in distilled water overnight to remove the solvent, then isolated and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum overnight.
Fe-MIL-100 was synthesized the same approach as described previously with some modifications [34]. A certain molar ratio of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}$ ·9H2O, and $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBTC}$ was dissolved in deionized water. After adding $3.24\mathrm{~ml}$ hydrofluoric acid, the solution was stirred magnetically for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ transferred to a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave, and heated at $\textcircled{160}\textcircled{{\circ}}{\mathrm C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the products were washed with deionized water and ethanol, centrifuged for 20 minutes, and dried at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
## 2.3 Characterization of Fe-MILs
The crystal structure and phase purity of the synthesized catalysts were recorded by X-ray diffraction (XRD) using a Rigaku D/Max 2500  equipped with Cu-K radiation (40 Kv, 250 mA), employing a scanning rate of $0.1^{\circ}$ $\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ in the range from 5 to $80^{\circ}$ . The particle morphology and crystal size were characterized by using scanning electron microscope (SEM, QUANTA F250, USA). Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) was recorded on a spectrometer (NICOLET 5700 FT-IR) using the standard KBr disk method to study the composition and structure of the catalyst. The UV–vis absorption spectra data were obtained from a UV–vis spectrometer (UV-4100, Hitachi, Japan) within the test range of $200{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}$ to test the light absorption properties. To determine the chemical states of elements, the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (ESCALAB 250XI, Thermo Fisher Scientific,
USA) was employed. The specific surface area was measured by Brunauer-Emmett-Teller method. The $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ physical absorption measurements were carried out at $77~\mathrm{K}$ on ASAP2020HD88. The detection of $\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2^{-}}$ , •OH and $\mathrm{\Deltah^{+}}$ was analyzed by electron spin resonance measurements (ESR, JEOL JES FA200, Japan) under visible light irradiation, with 5,5-dimethyl-l-pyrroline N-oxide being used as trapping agent.
## 2.4 Adsorption experiments
To optimize the adsorption time of the three Fe-MILs, the following batch test was performed. In this test, three reactors with a working volume of $200~\mathrm{mL}$ were carried out. Each reactor first received $100~\mathrm{\mL}$ of tetracycline medium $\mathrm{50~mg/L})$ , as prepared above. Then, the reactor was added with $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of synthesized Fe-MIL-101, Fe-MIL-100, or Fe-MIL-53. In the literature, catalyst dosage range is usually in the range of $0.1\mathrm{-}1\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{L}$ [35, 36], therefore, in order to guarantee the comparison experiments of the removal effect performed under the same conditions, thus $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of the catalyst was chosen as the dosage. The reactors were put into dark to prevent the possible illumination, and magnetic stirring (1000 rpm) was used to reach homogenization state of the suspensions. The test was lasted for $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ . At given time interval, $4~\mathrm{mL}$ of the samples were collected from each reactor into $5\mathrm{ml}$ centrifuge tubes, centrifuged at $8000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and filtrated by using $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ PTFE syringe filters. The residual concentration of tetracycline in the filtrate was measured by a UV–vis spectrophotometer (UV-2250, SHIMADZU Corporation, Japan) with the maximal adsorption wavelength of tetracycline at $357\mathrm{nm}$ .
## 2.5 Photocatalytic experiment
Photocatalytic activity of the Fe-based MOFs were implemented by the photocatalytic decomposition of tetracycline under visible light illumination $\lambda>420\mathrm{nm}$ ) after adsorption process. A 300 W Xenon lamp with a 420 nm cutoff filter was used as the visible light source. For the photocatalytic decomposition experiments, in our preliminary studies, we found that most of the tetracycline was adsorbed at low concentrations, so it could not identify the photocatalytic performance. Therefore, the high concentration of tetracycline could make the catalytic performances better detected and been contrasted, consequently, we chose $\mathrm{50mg/L}$ of tetracycline in the contrastive photocatalytic experiments. $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of the photocatalyst was chosen and added to $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ tetracycline aqueous solution as the photocatalytic reactor. Then the mixed solution was stirred in the dark ( $\mathrm{1000~rpm)}$ for 60 min to reach adsorption-desorption equilibrium between photocatalyst and tetracycline molecules. After adsorption, the reactor was transferred under visible light irradiation conditions, the photocatalytic process was lasted for $3\mathrm{~h~}$ .
The tetracycline concentrations after adsorption and during photo-degradation process were analyzed as above. What’s more, to test the durability and recyclability of the material, the recycle experiments were also put into effect. After each cycle, the solid photocatalyst was filtrated and washed for several times to remove the left pollutants by using distilled water and ethanol, and then dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight, preparing for the next cycle.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization of Fe-MILs
X-ray diffraction patterns of the synthesized Fe-MILs are shown in Fig. 1. It can be seen that the peaks of Fe-MIL-101 located at $2\Theta=8.9$ , 9.2, 10.6, 19.5 and $21.5^{\circ}$ . The pattern shows that Fe-MIL-101 has crystal phase structure, corresponding with the former literatures [37]. Besides, no impurity peaks were observed, indicating the high purity of the synthesized samples. The XRD patterns of the as-prepared product confirm that a high-quality pure phase of MIL-100(Fe) has been obtained as the diffraction peaks is consistent with the reported ones [38]. As for Fe-MIL-53,the characteristic diffraction peaks of it located at 9.2, 12.2, 18.4 and $28.0^{\circ}$ , which was in good agreement with the MIL-53 framework in the literature [33], indicating that Fe-MIL-53 was successfully synthesized.
Fig. 2 displayed the SEM images of several Fe-MILs. Fig. 2(a) and (b) show that the particles of Fe-MIL-101 have special microstructure and morphology of the metal dielectric structure, the particles are regular octahedron, and the crystal has a uniform size, which is about $500\mathrm{nm}$ . This is very similar to the previous reports [17, 29, 39].
For the picture of Fe-MIL-100, many small particles looks like granules, gathering together, most of the particles ’ sizes are different, around $500\ \mathrm{nm}\cdot\mathrm{1}\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ . The image of Fe-MIL-53 is like a bundle of wooden sticks with many different lengths sticks crisscross them, while the diameter of one bundle is about $80\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Besides, its surface is very smooth. This structure resembles with the reported ones [33, 40].
To analyze the elemental composition and electronic structure of Fe-MIL-101, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) were investigated. The XPS survey spectrum and high-resolution spectra of are shown in Fig. 3. Fig. 3(a) demonstrates that Fe-MIL-101 consists of Fe, C, and O elements. Fig. 3(b) shows high-resolution XPS spectrum of the C 1s, which could be fitted two surface components, corresponding to the carbon components on the benzoic at binding energy of $284.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ and the carboxylate $scriptstyle\mathbf{()-C=O},$ groups of the terephthalate linkers at binding energy of $288.7\mathrm{eV}$ respectively [41, 42]. In Fig. 3(c), The high-resolution XPS spectrum of the O 1s could be well accord with a peak at binding energies of around $531.8\ \mathrm{eV},$ which are attributed to the Fe-O bonds of Fe-MIL-101 [43]. Fig. 3 (d) described the high-resolution XPS spectrum of the Fe 2p, one binding energy peak is around $711.5\mathrm{eV},$ which is assigned to Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and the other peak around $726.0\mathrm{eV}$ is ascribed to Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ . This peak separation is calculated to be $14.5\ \mathrm{eV},$ this is very similar to those reported for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ [44].All of these results confirm the formation of Fe-MIL-101structure clearly.
## 3.2 Tetracycline removal performances of Fe-MILs
Before visible light irradiation, a dark adsorption experiment was carried out to investigate the adsorption-desorption equilibrium. As revealed in Fig. S1, For Fe-MIL-53, it was apparent that the adsorption equilibrium reached just in $10\ \mathrm{\min}$ . After $60\ \mathrm{\min}$ , the adsorption equilibrium of Fe-MIL-100 also reached. Moreover, it was suggested that the adsorption-desorption equilibrium between Fe-MIL-101 and tetracycline molecules had been attained at $40\ \mathrm{min}$ . Accordingly, for the photocatalytic experiments, before visible light irradiation, $60~\mathrm{\min}$ of dark adsorption was applied before the photodegradation process. To compare the photocatalytic activity of several Fe-MILs as integrated photocatalytic adsorbent, photodegradation experiments of tetracycline have been carried out under the same light illumination conditions after dark adsorption. The $C/C_{O}$ is employed to describe the degradation process ( $C$ is the tetracycline concentration at time t and $C_{\theta}$ is the initial concentration of tetracycline: $50~\mathrm{\mg/L})$ . As is shown in Fig. 4, tetracycline could be hardly degraded by Fe-MIL-100 under visible light, with no significant change of degradation during the photocatalytic process. As for Fe-MIL-53, after adsorbing $10.5\%$ of tetracycline, about $30.2\%$ of tetracycline was removed during the visible light irradiation process, reaching $40.7\%$ in total, while Fe-MIL-101 showed much higher activity than the other two kinds of Fe-MILs under the visible light exposure. It was clearly indicated that the Fe-MIL-101 exhibits the excellent performance in the degradation of tetracycline. In the first hour under irradiation, the degradation rate was not very fast, and approximately $10.8\%$ of the tetracycline was degraded. In the next hour, more than $30.5\%$ of the tetracycline was degraded, reaching $87\%$ of the total tetracycline. After irradiation, $96.6\%$ of the total tetracycline was removed after $3\mathrm{~h~}$ irradiation.
## 3.3 Adsorption performances
As revealed in Fig. 4, for Fe-MIL-53, it was apparent that the material adsorbed about only $10.5\%$ of tetracycline when reached the adsorption equilibrium. The adsorption equilibrium of Fe-MIL-100 reached about
$44.1\%$ percent of tetracycline. For Fe-MIL-101, the material exhibited the highest tetracycline adsorption removal efficiency, reaching about $55.1\%$ . There was no significant increasing after $40\mathrm{min}$ , as the repulsive forces between the adsorbed tetracycline molecules and existed molecules in the solution increases [45].
## 3.4 Adsorption mechanism of Fe-MILs
## 3.4.1 Adsorption equilibrium
The experiments of tetracycline adsorption on Fe-MILs were carried out with five initial concentrations: 10, 20, 30, 40 and $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ at room temperature. To understand the adsorption process and compare the materials’ adsorption abilities over tetracycline, the adsorption isotherm models were utilized. In this work, the Langmuir and Freundlich isotherm equations were used to model the adsorption data. The equation of the Langmuir isotherm is as follows:
$$
\frac{\mathrm{C_{e}}}{\mathrm{q_{e}}}=\frac{1}{\mathrm{q_{m}k_{1}}}+\frac{\mathrm{C_{e}}}{\mathrm{q_{m}}}
$$
This equation assumes that the adsorbent surface has a uniform adsorption capacity, there is no interaction between the adsorbed molecules, only forming monolayer adsorption on the adsorbent surface. In this equation, $c_{e}$ is the equilibrium concentration of tetracycline in the solution, $\mathrm{mg/L}$ . $q_{e}\mathrm{~,~}q_{m}$ are the equilibrium and the maximum amount of tetracycline adsorbed on the three MILs, $\mathrm{mg/g}$ . And $k_{I}$ is the Langmuir constant, represents the strength of the adsorption capacity.
The Freundlich isotherm is an empirical formula without any assumptions. The equation is commonly expressed in logarithmic form:
$$
\mathrm{\lgq=\frac{1}{n}l g C+l g k_{f}}
$$
Where $k_{f}$ is the Freundlich adsorption constant which reflects the adsorption ability, $1/n$ is an index, indicating the degree of difficulty of adsorption.
The fitting linear of Langmuir and Freundlich models of the tetracycline adsorptions were shown in Fig. 5. It can be seen from Fig. 5 that the adsorption of tetracycline onto Fe-MIL-100 and Fe-MIL-53 can be fitted by the Langmuir model as the correlation coefficient of $R^{2}$ is 0.9849, which was higher than those of the Freundlich model, the Langmuir isotherm is based on monolayer sorption onto the sorbent surface which contain a limited number of adsorptive sites with uniform energies, indicating that the surfaces of Fe-MIL-100 and Fe-MIL-53 distributes uniform functional groups and the process of adsorbing tetracycline was monolayer molecular adsorption over homogenous adsorbent surface. Particularly, for Fe-MIL-101, the experiment data was fitted better by the Freundlich model, relating a heterogeneous energy distribution of active sites on adsorbents. The Freundlich parameter $\mathrm{\tilde{n}}=1.717>1$ ) also showed that the adsorption condition was favorable [46].
## 3.4.2 Adsorption kinetics
In order to understand the adsorption process of tetracycline onto Fe-MILs, the pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order were used at five different initial tetracycline concentrations. The first-order and second-order model equations are as follows:
$$
\scriptstyle\log(q_{\mathrm{e}^{-}}q_{\mathrm{t}})=\log q_{\mathrm{e}^{-}}\mathbf{k}_{1}\mathbf{t}
$$
$$
\cfrac{t}{q_{t}}=\cfrac{1}{k_{2}{q_{e}}^{2}}+\cfrac{t}{q_{e}}
$$
Among them, $q_{e}$ and $q_{t}$ are the amount of adsorption at the equilibrium time and the amount of adsorption at time $t,k_{I}$ is the pseudo first order kinetic constant and $k_{2}$ stands for the second order kinetic constant .The results of linear fittings are shown in Fig. S2-4. The kinetic parameters and calculated adsorption rate are listed in Table S1-3. Comparing the correlation coefficients $(R^{2}),$ the experimental data by the pseudo-second order model modifies better as it shows more higher correlation coefficient values (all $R^{2}>0.999\$ ). Besides, the experimental $q_{e}$ values $(q_{e},$ exp) are connected to the calculated q values $(q_{e,\ c a l})$ calculated from the pseudo-second-order model than that counted by the pseudo-first-order model. The first-order kinetic adsorption model assumes that the adsorption process is mainly controlled by the diffusion step while the second-order kinetic adsorption model assumes that the adsorption process is mainly affected by the chemical adsorption in the surface adsorption process. In the step of surface adsorption, the electron sharing or electron transfer between adsorbates and adsorbents leads to chemical adsorption. These experimental results indicated that the adsorption process of Fe-MIL-101, Fe-MIL-100 and Fe-MIL-53 were not the result of single factor and mainly dominated by chemical adsorption [47].
## 3.4.3 FTIR and BET analysis
FTIR spectroscopy was used to study the structural integrity of Fe-MIL-101 and the interactions between the catalyst and tetracycline molecules. As is displayed in Fig. 6, The characteristic absorption peaks of the Fe-MIL-101 were observed at 1680, 1583, 1396, 1020, and $750~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which could mainly originate from the vibrations of carboxylate groups and are close to those reported in the previous literatures [29, 48]. A broad peak located at $3420~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is assigned to the stretching vibrations of the $\mathrm{O-H}$ from the surface adsorbed water. The two sharp peaks at 1583 and $1396~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ are associated with asymmetric (νs(C–O)) and symmetric (νs(C–O)) vibrations of carboxyl groups, respectively, suggesting the presence of the dicarboxylate linker within the sample. There exists a peak at $750\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , corresponding to $\mathrm{C-H}$ bending vibrations of the benzene [49]. After adsorption, the main peaks of Fe-MIL-101 have no change, indicating that the Fe-MIL-101 was stable under the reaction conditions. Additionally, the intensity of peaks at 1680, 1396, $750~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ enhanced, indicating the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ , C-O, C-H may be the adsorption sites. The adsorption was due to the increased hydrogen bonding between the [Fe(OH)] groups of the Fe-MIL-101 and nitrogen atom of tetracycline [50]. Besides, in this reaction system, Fe-MIL-101 with benzene rings and the phenolic hydroxyl groups of tetracycline interact on each other, the phenolic hydroxyl groups of tetracycline was a charge donor and Fe-MIL-101 could be charge acceptor, thus formatting a strong π-π donor-receptor system [51].
To explain the reasons for the differences in tetracycline adsorption properties of different materials, the BET analysis was put into effect. The porous structures of the synthesized Fe-MILs were analyzed using ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherm measurement. As showed in Table 1, the BET surface areas of Fe-MIL-101, Fe-MIL-100 and Fe-MIL-53 were calculated to be 252.59, 1203.36 and $21.42~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively. Compared with the other two Fe-MILs, Fe-MIL-101 possessed the highest pore volume and largest pore size, which may enhanced its adsorption property.
In Fig. 7, the Fe-MIL-100 presented a typeⅠisotherm, indicating the microporous structure existed in the material. The adsorption capacity raised rapidly with the increase of the relative pressure, which was due to the effect of micropore filling. For Fe-MIL-101, the curve has no inflection point, the higher relative pressure was, the larger adsorption amount was observed, which indicated that there stands hole filling. The adsorption capacity of Fe-MIL-53 was relatively small, which might be related to the smaller specific surface area.
## 3.5 Photocatalytic process analysis of Fe-MILs
## 3.5.1 Effect of initial concentrations of tetracycline
For the treatment of practical wastewater, the initial pollutant concentration is an important factor to achieve commendable removal effect. The effect of initial tetracycline concentrations was investigated with different initial concentrations in the range from 10 to $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ , and the results were illustrated in Fig. 8. It was obvious that the initial tetracycline concentration played a significant role in adsorption process, where $88.3\%$ , $81.8\%$ , $61.2\%$ ,
$69.0\%$ and $66.0\%$ of tetracycline were removed at the initial tetracycline concentration accord with 10, 20, 30, 40 and $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ , respectively. The Fe-MIL-101 removed most of the tetracycline at relatively low initial concentration after 1 hour adsorption, which has great significance in the industrial application, the removal efficiency decreased with the increasing of initial tetracycline concentrations, and the removal efficiency decreased from primary $88.2\%$ $\scriptstyle{C_{0}=10\ \mathrm{mg/L}}$ to later $66.0\%$ ( $C_{0}{=}50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ ) after 1 hour irradiation. This phenomenon was coincident with the former literature [52]. First of all, with higher tetracycline concentrations, the path length of photon entering the tetracycline solution would be decreased [13]. Moreover, other intermediates could be generated by tetracycline molecules during the photocatalytic process, thus competing with tetracycline molecules for the adsorption and photocatalytic sites on the surface of the catalyst [53]. In addition, due to the fact that under the same conditions, equal amount of reactive radicals generated in all solutions; with increasing the initial  concentration of tetracycline, the reaction between tetracycline molecules and radicals becomes more likely at lower tetracycline concentrations, thus the degradation rate was significantly reduced [54]. It was notable that Fe-MIL-101 material also displayed relatively high photocatalytic activity towards tetracycline degradation, after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ irradiation, only about $9.1\%$ loss from $10~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ to $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ , indicating that Fe-MIL-101 possessed great potential for tetracycline or other refractory pollutants removal at relatively higher concentration.
## 3.5.2 The effect of Fe-MIL-101 dosages
Effect of Fe-MIL-101dosages in the range $(0.1\mathrm{-}0.7~\mathrm{g/L})$ ) was evaluated for removal experiments. As shown in Fig. 9, the tests were performed at the tetracycline concentration of $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ . The results showed that the removal efficiency of tetracycline increased when adding more Fe-MIL-101, the removal efficiency of tetracycline was $32.7\%$ and $55.0\%$ (at doses of $0.1\ \mathrm{g/L}$ and $0.5~\mathrm{g/L}\$ ) after $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ adsorption. But when the dosage was $0.7~\mathrm{g/L}$ , the adsorption efficiency slightly declined, reaching $51.5\%$ . As shown in Fig. 9, in the first 1h irradiation, there was no obvious difference when the adding amount was $0.5~\ \mathrm{g/L}$ or $0.7~\ \mathrm{g/L}$ . However, during the next hour, the photocatalytic degradation efficiency increased rapidly with adding $0.5~\mathrm{g/L}$ catalysts. The effect was much better than the dosage of $0.7\ \mathrm{g/L}$ . This can be explained from two aspects, Firstly, with more catalysts, the turbidity of the solution raises, which may make the light dispersed and reduce the influence of light through the solution [14, 55]. Secondly, under larger catalyst adding conditions, the Fe-MIL-101 precipitated and accumulated, thus the production of active species in the photocatalytic procedure may be reduced [56]. Considering the removal efficiency and economy, the Fe-MIL-101 dosage of $0.5\mathrm{g/L}$ was chosen as the optimum dosage in the successive
experiments.
## 3.5.3 Photocatalyst recyclability
In the practical applications of the catalysts, recyclability is an important factor [57]. To investigate this ability, the same amount of Fe-MIL-101 catalysts was gathered after each cycle by washing and centrifugation, reused in the next adsorption and photocatalytic reaction process for three times under the equal experimental conditions. As shown in Fig. 10, after repeated use, the adsorption effect and photocatalytic effect of the material had ignorable decrease, indicating good recyclability in terms of adsorption and photocatalytic tetracycline degradation effect of Fe-MIL-101 during recycles. Moreover, as can be seen from Fig. 1, there was no obvious change in the XRD curve after reaction compared with that before the reaction, which indicated that the material has good stability. In conclusion, Fe-MIL-101 exhibited high stability and recyclability for the photocatalytic degradation of tetracycline, which provides great potential for the practical treatment of refractory pollutants.
## 3.5.4 Kinetics of photocatalytic degradation of tetracycline
In order to further investigate and understand the kinetics of photo-catalytic degradation of tetracycline by Fe-MILs, analysis were carried out under optimal conditions (Fe-MILs dosed $0.5~\mathrm{g/L}$ , initial concentration of tetracycline was $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ , contact time 180 min). According to the previous reports, pseudo-first order kinetics was used to describe photo-catalytic degradation of antibiotics pollutants [38, 51]. In this study, the above model was used to describe the tetracycline photo-catalytic degradation. The equation is:
$$
\mathrm{ln}{\frac{C_{0}}{C_{t}}}=\bf k_{0}t
$$
In this equation, $C_{0}$ is the initial concentration of tetracycline after adsorption, $C_{t}$ is the concentration of tetracycline at time $t$ during photocatalytic process, $k_{\theta}$ stands for the pseudo-first-order reaction rate constant $(\mathrm{min^{-1}})$ , and t is the photocatalytic reaction time (min). The photocatalytic reaction rate constant $(k)$ could be calculated from the pseudo-first order kinetic curve.
The calculation results of Fe-MILs are presented in Table 2. As is shown, the values of the coefficients $(\boldsymbol{R}^{2})$ of Fe-MIL-101 and Fe-MIL-53 were close to ‘1’, this showed that the kinetics pseudo-first-order model was an appropriate model to describe the reaction rate. For Fe-MIL-100, the coefficient $(\boldsymbol{R}^{2})$ was 0.5356, which may due to its poor performance during visible light. In Fig. 11, comparing the constant degradation rate of $\mathbf{k}$ , it could be seen that Fe-MIL-101 possessed the highest photocatalytic degradation rate, which was in good accordance with the experimental results. This might due to the reason that Fe-MIL-101 adsorbed the most amounts of tetracycline, thus providing more feasible reaction active sites during the photocatalytic process.
## 3.5.5 UV-vis analysis
In order to explain the different tetracycline degradation performances of several Fe-MILs, the light absorption properties and optical bandgap of the several materials were investigated by UV/vis diffuse reflectance spectrum. As shown in Fig. 12, Fe-MIL-101 exhibited the strongest visible light range absorption, indicating it has great potential capacity for visible-light harvesting. Moreover, the band gap energy of a semiconductor catalyst is a vital optical capability index to evaluate the formation and transfer of holes and electrons during illumination [58]. The absorption edge of Fe-MIL-101, Fe-MIL-100 and Fe-MIL-53 is about $660~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , 601 nm and $630\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to their band gaps of about 1.88 eV, 2.06 eV and $1.97\ \mathrm{eV}.$ The absorption of light would be stronger with the smaller bandgap, the results show that the Fe-MIL-101 has a very efficient utilization of visible light during the photocatalytic process, thus possessing the most excellent performances of degrading tetracycline.
## 3.6 The species and role of active radical during photocatalytic tetracycline degradation
In order to investigate the origin of degrading tetracycline by Fe-MIL-101, the main active species formed during the reaction process were identified by hole and free radical trapping experiments under the same visible light conditions. In the previous study, 1.4-benzoquinone (BQ) was utilized for superoxide radical $(\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2^{-}})$ scavenger, ethylenediamintetraacetic acid disodium (EDTA-2Na) and Methanol were used as hole $\left(\mathrm{h}^{+}\right)$ scavenger and hydroxyl radical (•OH) scavenger [21, 59]. Besides, ${\bf N}_{2}$ purging experiment was also implemented to testify the role of $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{O}_{2}.$ - in the photocatalytic system. The experimental results (Fig. 13) showed obvious inhibitory degradation effect during $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ purging. The removal efficiency of tetracycline declined from $96.6\%$ (air-equilibrated condition) to $73.2\%$ with $\bar{\bf N}_{2}$ purging, which demonstrated the significant function of $\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2^{-}}$ in the photocatalytic process. Similarly, the photocatalytic activity was almost inhibited by methanol and the degradation efficiency of tetracycline reduced from $99.6\%$ to $70.4\%$ .indicating that •OH played an important role in the reaction process. Hence, the major active species should be $\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2^{-}}$ , •OH.
However, when adding $\mathrm{\hslash}^{+}$ trapping agent like EDTA-2Na, Sodium oxalate, ammonium oxalate and triethanolamine, the catalyst was reacted, when adding 1.4-benzoquinone, it affected the detection of tetracycline, therefore, to investigate the effect of $\mathrm{h}^{+}$ , the ESR spin-trap experiment with DMPO technique was also studied. As indicated in Fig. 14A, there was no signal of $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{O}_{2}.$ -  in the dark, while under the light irradiation for $5\mathrm{min}$ , the signals of DMPO- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ - could be detected, under the visible light for $10~\mathrm{min}$ , the intensity of $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{O}_{2}.$ - increased more significantly, confirming the increase of $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{}_{\mathbf{}}(\mathbf{\cdot}_{2}$ - for the photocatalytic performance. In addition, there was no signal of •OH in the dark, however, the signal of •OH could be observed when exposed to the light (Fig. 14B). After irradiation from 5 min to $10~\mathrm{min}$ , the peaks intensity enhanced greatly, suggesting the forming of •OH during the photocatalytic process. What is more, the enhanced intensity of $\mathrm{h}^{+}$ was detected under visible light for 5min (Fig. 14C), revealing that $\mathrm{{h^{+}}}$ also played an important role for the degradation of tetracycline. Therefore, the radicals trap experiments and ESR analysis showed that the photocatalytic process was governed by the jointly action of $\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{\Deltah^{+}}$ and •OH.
Due to the existence of extensive iron oxo clusters cross-linked by ligands in the structure, under visible light irradiations, the Fe-O clusters would be excited directly, making Fe-based MOF materials responsive to visible light [60, 61]. Additionally, the ESR spin-trap experiments of Fe-MIL-100 and Fe-MIL-53 were also tested, it was shown in Fig. 15 and Fig. 16 that $\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2^{-}}$ , •OH and $\mathrm{\h}^{+}$ were all detected after irradiation and enhanced with the increase of time, indicating $\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{\Deltah^{+}}$ and •OH also played important roles during the photocatalytic degradation process of Fe-MIL-100 and Fe-MIL-53. Besides, as shown in Fig. 17, under visible-light irradiation, on the one hand, the photo-excited electrons on the valence band (VB) of Fe-MILs move to their conduction bands (CB). In the $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{CB}}$ of Fe-MILs, the photogenerated electrons could reduce the adsorbed $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to $\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2^{-}}$ . On the other hand, •OH can be generated by the reaction between the photoexcited holes on the VB of Fe-MILs and the surface-adsorbed $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . In addition, the generated holes can oxidize the tetracycline molecules. Consequently, the formed active species, $\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2^{-}}$ , •OH and $\mathrm{h}^{+}$ , could effectively degrade the tetracycline into small intermediates or end products directly.
## 4 Conclusions
In this work, three kinds of Fe-MILs have been successfully synthesized. It was found that Fe-MIL-101 displayed the most excellent adsorption performance among the three Fe-MILs. Except the good adsorption ability, Fe-MIL-101 also exhibited excellent photocatalytic degradation performance when the initial concentration of tetracycline was 50 mg/L. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET), UV–vis absorption spectra analysis revealed that Fe-MIL-101 possessed large surface area, pore sizes, pore volumes and the strongest visible light adsorption ability. The Fe-MIL-101 also displayed good stability and recyclability after three adsorption/photodegradation cycles. The trapping experiments and ESR tests confirmed that the $\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}.$ , $\mathrm{\Deltah^{+}}$ and •OH were main active species in photocatalytic degradation of tetracycline. The present study may provide a new potential for Fe-based MILs in the practical treatments of tetracycline or other refractory antibiotics.
